As the team was in the process of setting up a new Jenkins server, where they were adding agent nodes to handle workloads. 
They want to add a label for Jenkins master itself. Find below more details about the same.

Add a label kke-jenkins for Jenkins master.

Click the Jenkins button on the top bar to open the Jenkins UI. Log in using username admin and password Adm!n321.

SOLUTION
To add a label to the Jenkins master (now referred to as the Built-In Node in modern Jenkins versions), you can do this directly from the Jenkins UI.

🛠️ Step-by-Step Guide
Log in to Jenkins: Click the Jenkins button on the top bar and log in using the credentials:

Username: admin

Password: Adm!n321

Navigate to Node Management: From the Jenkins dashboard, go to Manage Jenkins → Manage Nodes and Clouds (or Manage Nodes in some versions).

Select the Built-In Node: You will see a list of nodes. The master node is typically named Built-In Node (older versions called it master). Click on it to select it.

Open Configuration: Click the Configure button (or the downward arrow next to the node name and select Configure).

Add the Label: In the configuration page, locate the Labels field. Enter the label kke-jenkins exactly as required. Note that if you need to add multiple labels, they should be separated by spaces.

Save the Configuration: Scroll down and click Save to apply the changes.

✅ Verification
After saving, you will be returned to the node list. The new label kke-jenkins should now be visible next to the Built-In Node.

💡 Note for Newer Jenkins Versions
In Jenkins versions 2.307 and later, the default master node was officially renamed to Built-In Node, and the implicit master label was removed. Manually adding a label like kke-jenkins (or master if needed for legacy jobs) is the standard workaround to ensure jobs can target the controller correctly.


QUESTION 2:
After setting up the Jenkins server, the Nautilus team was analysing some security settings w.r.t the same. They wanted to disable the functionality to remember the login for a user so that user will have to login each time to the Jenkins server. They came up with below requirements for the same.
Turn on the Disable “Keep me signed in” option in Jenkins configuration.

Click the Jenkins button on the top bar to open the Jenkins UI. Log in using username admin and password Adm!n321.

SOLUTION
To disable the "Keep me signed in" functionality for all users, you can modify the global security settings in the Jenkins UI. This setting removes the checkbox from the login page, ensuring that users must authenticate each time they access the instance.

🛠️ Step-by-Step Guide
Log in to Jenkins: Click the Jenkins button on the top bar and log in using the credentials:

Username: admin

Password: Adm!n321

Navigate to Security Configuration: From the Jenkins dashboard, go to Manage Jenkins → Configure Global Security (or Configure Security depending on your version).

Locate and Enable the Option: Scroll through the security settings to find the checkbox labeled Disable remember me (or Disable "Keep me signed in" in some versions). Check this box.

Save the Configuration: Click Save at the bottom of the page to apply the changes.

✅ Verification
After saving, log out of Jenkins and return to the login screen. The "Keep me signed in" checkbox will no longer be displayed, and users will be required to enter their credentials every time they access the Jenkins server.

💡 Note
This option has been available since Jenkins v1.534 and is the standard method to disable the remember-me cookie functionality. If you are using a newer version, the label might appear as Disable "Keep me signed in" to match the login page wording.

