QUESTION 6:
The Nautilus DevOps team want to install and setup some Jenkins plugins which are needed by some of the jobs they are going to create. Recently they have shared below requirements.


Click on the Jenkins button on the top bar to access the Jenkins UI. Login using username admin and Adm!n321 password.

Install the Jenkins plugin Mailer, please keep it disabled for now. You might need to restart Jenkins service to install these plugins, so we recommend clicking on Restart Jenkins when installation is complete and no jobs are running on plugin installation/update page i.e update centre.

SOLUTION
To install the Jenkins Mailer plugin and keep it disabled, you will first use the Plugin Manager to install it, and then use the Installed tab to disable it. This approach ensures the plugin is present on the system for any dependencies but its functionality remains inactive until you choose to enable it.

🛠️ Step-by-Step Guide
Log in to Jenkins: Click the Jenkins button on the top bar and log in using the credentials:

Username: admin

Password: Adm!n321

Navigate to the Plugin Manager:

From the Jenkins dashboard, go to Manage Jenkins.

Click on Plugins (or Manage Plugins).

Install the Mailer Plugin:

Open the Available tab.

In the search box, type Mailer.

Locate the Mailer Plugin in the results and check the box next to it.

Click the "Install without restart" button at the bottom of the page. This installs the plugin and allows it to be managed immediately without an immediate Jenkins restart.

Disable the Plugin:

After installation, switch to the Installed tab in the Plugin Manager.

In the search box (if available) or by scrolling, find the Mailer plugin.

You will see a checkbox in the Enabled column to the right of the plugin name.

Uncheck this checkbox to disable the plugin.

Restart Jenkins (Optional but Recommended):

While the "Install without restart" option makes the plugin visible for configuration, some changes may require a restart to fully take effect. You can restart Jenkins by checking the "Restart Jenkins when installation is complete and no jobs are running" option on the installation page, or by navigating to JENKINS_URL/restart. After a restart, the disabled state will be firmly applied.

💡 Important Note on Dependencies
Be aware that the Mailer plugin is a dependency for many other plugins (such as Git, GitLab, and Email Extension). If you later install or update plugins that depend on Mailer, Jenkins may automatically re-enable it. If this happens, you can simply return to the Installed tab and uncheck the "Enabled" box again to keep it disabled.

