QUESTION 8:
It's a common issue in some Jenkins configurations where the "Email Address" field doesn't appear on the user creation form. This is almost always because the Mailer plugin, which provides this field, is either not installed, or as you did in a previous task, it has been disabled.

Here are a few ways to resolve this and attach the email to the admin2 user.

✅ Solution 1: Enable the Mailer Plugin (Recommended)
The most direct fix is to ensure the Mailer plugin is active. In a previous task, you were asked to install it but keep it disabled, which is likely the root cause here.

Go to Manage Jenkins > Plugins.

Switch to the Installed tab.

Find the Mailer plugin in the list.

Check the box in the Enabled column to activate it.

Restart Jenkins for the change to take full effect.

After Jenkins restarts, try creating the user again; the Email Address field should now be visible.

⚙️ Solution 2: Add Email via Jenkins Script Console
If you prefer not to restart or the UI still doesn't cooperate, you can add the email address programmatically. This method bypasses the UI form entirely.

Go to Manage Jenkins > Script Console.

Paste the following Groovy script, replacing the email address with the one for admin2:

groovy
import hudson.model.User
import hudson.tasks.Mailer

def user = User.get('admin2', false)
if (user != null) {
    user.addProperty(new Mailer.UserProperty('admin2@example.com'))
    user.save()
    println "Email updated for admin2"
} else {
    println "User admin2 not found"
}
Click Run. The script will attach the specified email address to the user account.

📝 Solution 3: Manually Edit the User's Configuration File
This is a more hands-on approach that involves editing the user's config.xml file directly on the Jenkins server.

Create the user admin2 through the normal UI (even without the email field). This creates the necessary user directory.

SSH into the Jenkins server.

Navigate to the user's configuration directory: $JENKINS_HOME/users/admin2/. The JENKINS_HOME is typically /var/lib/jenkins or /var/jenkins_home.

Open the config.xml file in a text editor.

Find or add the <emailAddress> tag and set its value to admin2@example.com:

xml
<emailAddress>admin2@example.com</emailAddress>
Save the file and restart the Jenkins service for the change to be recognized.

💡 Final Recommendation
For this specific KodeKloud task, Solution 1 (enabling the Mailer plugin) is the most appropriate and permanent fix. It aligns with standard Jenkins setup, and once the plugin is enabled, the email field will be available for all future user creations without the need for manual scripting or file edits.


